## Pillar 1 — Data Source & Mapping Integrity

Context: Asana rollup "Pillar 1 · Data Source & Mapping Integrity" (v0.8.0,
3 subtasks). Closes the data-source/mapping lifecycle gaps surfaced by real
usage: removing a metadata table silently orphaned the mappings that read from
it, "Update from UC" overwrote the stored schema with no preview and no undo,
and a column renamed or dropped upstream stayed invisible until a build failed.

Two findings from the code changed the shape of the work versus the roadmap.
First, the roadmap's claim that the metadata-refresh column diff was "already
computed" is wrong — `merge_table_metadata` had both column lists in scope and
discarded the comparison, so the diff is net-new (small and pure, so the
estimate held). Second, the deletion guard was cheaper than estimated:
`Mapping._collect_source_tables()` already returned `{table: [referrer]}`
covering explicit triples, relationship source/target tables, and FQNs parsed
out of freeform SQL — the hard half was done. Decisions taken with the
requester: the guard is confirm-with-detail (not a hard block), and the drift
warning ships on both Diagnostics and the designer canvas.

Changes:

1. src/back/objects/domain/Domain.py
   New pure `compute_column_diff(old_columns, new_columns)` returning
   `added`/`removed`/`type_changed`/`unchanged`, plus `has_column_changes`.
   Shared by the refresh preview and (indirectly) the drift work. A rename is
   reported as remove+add — `DESCRIBE` gives no identity to correlate the two,
   and guessing would produce confident-but-wrong previews. Extracted
   `_column_name` / `_column_type` to handle the `col_name`/`name` and
   `data_type`/`type` key split in one place; `merge_table_metadata` now uses
   the former instead of repeating the fallback inline.
2. src/back/objects/mapping/Mapping.py
   New `find_mappings_referencing(tables)` wrapping `_collect_source_tables()`,
   matching on the full triple or (when the UI supplies a short name) on the
   table segment alone, case-insensitively. Returns only tables that are still
   in use, so an empty result means the removal is safe.
3. src/back/objects/mapping/Mapping.py
   Extracted `_entity_source_triples` / `_relationship_source_triples` and
   rewrote `_collect_source_tables` on top of them, removing the duplicated
   triple-extraction logic now that the drift checks need the same per-mapping
   view. The relationship helper tags each triple with its side
   (source/target/sql) so both the diagnostics labels and the side-specific
   drift check can reuse it.
4. src/api/routers/internal/domain.py
   New `POST /domain/metadata/removal-impact` — read-only pre-flight returning
   the referrer map so the UI can list what a removal would break.
5. src/back/objects/domain/Domain.py
   `get_removal_impact` (route delegate) and `_invalidate_on_removal`.
   `save_metadata_tables` / `clear_metadata` now compute which tables actually
   disappeared and, when any were still referenced, call
   `clear_generated_content()` + `record_change()` before saving — matching what
   every mapping mutator already did, and which these two were alone in
   skipping. Stale R2RML must not outlive the table it describes. Both return
   the impact map so callers can report it.
6. src/front/static/global/js/utils.js
   `showConfirmDialog` gained optional `detailHtml` (scrollable bordered panel
   under the message), `size`, and `headerClass`. One generalization serves both
   new dialogs instead of two bespoke modals; existing callers are unaffected.
7. src/front/static/domain/js/domain-metadata.js
   `fetchRemovalImpact` / `buildRemovalImpactHtml` / `confirmRemovalWithImpact`,
   wired into `removeSelectedTables()` and `clearMetadata()`. With no impact the
   dialog is unchanged from today; with impact it becomes a danger-header
   modal listing each doomed table and its referrers. A failed pre-flight
   degrades to the plain confirm rather than blocking the removal.
8. src/back/objects/domain/_metadata_tasks.py
   `run_metadata_update_task` snapshots `old_table["columns"]` and computes the
   diff *before* calling `merge_table_metadata`, which replaces the list in
   place and makes the previous schema unrecoverable. The per-table diff (only
   tables that actually changed) is returned as a new `diff` key alongside the
   existing result keys.
9. src/back/objects/domain/Domain.py
   Same snapshot-then-diff in the synchronous `update_metadata_tables`, so both
   paths agree even though the UI only drives the async one.
10. src/front/static/domain/js/domain-metadata.js
    `buildColumnDiffHtml` / `confirmMetadataDiff`, and `monitorMetadataTask` now
    gates the previously automatic `/domain/metadata/save` behind the review
    dialog whenever the diff is non-empty. Declining discards the in-memory
    result — the correct rollback given `DomainSession.save()` is a full-blob
    overwrite with no undo. A no-op refresh still applies straight through.
11. src/back/objects/mapping/Mapping.py
    `_fetch_live_columns` (one `DESCRIBE` per distinct source table, not per
    attribute), `_drift_check`, `_entity_drift_checks`,
    `_relationship_drift_checks`. Drift is `warning`, not `error`: the mapping
    is well-formed, the upstream table moved. Tables the client cannot read
    return `[]` and are skipped rather than reported as fully dropped.
12. src/back/objects/mapping/Mapping.py
    `run_diagnostics` appends drift checks to each entity/relationship result,
    but only when `available_columns is None` — a parseable `SELECT` list is
    already validated against its own projection, whose aliases legitimately
    differ from the table's column names, so checking those against the live
    schema would be a false positive. `SELECT *` parses to `None` and therefore
    does get drift, which is the desired case. `_diagnose_relationship` now
    returns `available_columns` for symmetry with `_diagnose_entity`.
13. src/back/objects/mapping/Mapping.py
    `get_schema_drift(client)` — a cheap subset of `run_diagnostics` (no SELECT
    probes, no row counts) keyed by class/property URI, so the designer can flag
    drift on load without paying for a full diagnostics run.
14. src/api/routers/internal/mapping.py
    New `GET /mapping/schema-drift`.
15. src/front/static/mapping/js/mapping-diagnostics.js
    `_isDrift` / `_driftBadge`; drift rows get their own icon and a
    "(schema drift)" suffix, and affected items carry an "N drifted" badge in
    the collapsed header so it is visible without expanding.
16. src/front/static/mapping/js/mapping-design.js
    `MappingDriftState` + `loadSchemaDrift`, fetched in parallel with the map
    layout on designer init (advisory — a failure just means no markers). A ⚠
    glyph and tooltip on affected canvas nodes, a warning banner at the top of
    the entity and relationship panels listing the missing columns, and a badge
    on the bound column header when the column is still in the query output.
17. src/front/static/mapping/css/mapping-design.css
    `.mapping-map-node-drift` styling for the canvas marker.
18. documentation/user-guide.md
    New "Refreshing metadata (Update from UC)" and "Removing data sources"
    subsections under Import Table Metadata, and a "Source Schema Drift" section
    under Mapping covering the three surfaces and why aliased SQL is exempt.
19. documentation/data-access.md
    Three new rows in the Domain and Mapping feature/endpoint tables.
20. README.md
    New "Data source & mapping integrity (0.8.0)" section.
21. pyproject.toml
    Version 0.7.2 → 0.8.0 (the release this pillar targets, per the Asana Git
    Branch field).
22. tests/units/domain/test_column_diff.py
    New — 14 cases for `compute_column_diff` / `has_column_changes`: add,
    remove, rename-as-remove-plus-add, type change, comment-only change,
    empty/None sides, both key conventions, blank DESCRIBE separator rows.
23. tests/units/mapping/test_removal_impact.py
    New — 11 cases for the referrer lookup: explicit triple, sql_query-only,
    relationship source/target, multiple referrers, excluded mappings skipped,
    no-match, bare and back-ticked names, case-insensitivity, empty input.
24. tests/units/domain/test_metadata_removal_guard.py
    New — 11 cases covering `get_removal_impact` (including that it is
    read-only) and the invalidation behaviour of `save_metadata_tables` /
    `clear_metadata`: fires only when a *referenced* table is dropped, still
    persists the reduced set, and records a summarising change event.
25. tests/units/domain/test_metadata_refresh_diff.py
    New — 7 cases driving `run_metadata_update_task` with a fake client:
    added/removed columns, type changes, unchanged tables absent from the diff,
    the snapshot-before-merge ordering, multi-table, and result-key stability.
26. tests/units/mapping/test_schema_drift.py
    New — 18 cases: dropped id/label/attribute columns, no false positive on a
    matching schema, advisory severity, unreadable tables skipped, aliased SQL
    exempt but `SELECT *` covered, one fetch per table, excluded entities
    skipped, per-side relationship resolution, and `get_schema_drift` shape
    including the no-client and DESCRIBE-failure paths.
27. tests/e2e/domain/test_domain_api_flows.py
    Contract tests for `POST /domain/metadata/removal-impact`.
28. tests/e2e/mapping/test_mapping_api_flows.py
    Contract tests for `GET /mapping/schema-drift`.

Behaviour note: the deletion guard informs, it does not block — a user can still
remove a mapped data source, which is deliberate and consistent with the other
destructive metadata actions. The cost is that the generated R2RML/SQL is
cleared on such a removal, so the next build regenerates rather than shipping a
mapping that points at a table the domain no longer knows about.

